# ComposeUX Intelligence Console [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

> The HAL ([SPEC:CXC-HAL-DECLARE]) declares a $0.10 sensor onto the bus with a `config` block, `calibration`,
> and a role. This spec declares the largest peripheral there is — an Intelligence — the exact same way. Its
> bootstrap IS its config; its dusk gifts ARE its calibration; it is a `transactional` peer you message and it
> emits; its interface KIND is `channel`. Two moves make it more than "a Claude on the bus": it reads its OWN
> context back as a `self/*` sensor (the blind spot every silent compaction-loss hides behind, un-blinded), and
> it OWNS its own lifecycle end-to-end — it decides when to cross the seam, mints its own dusk gifts, and authors
> a custom `/compact` a dumb launcher types back into its stdin. Intent flows up the bus; the mechanism is a
> finger on a key at the leaf. The Intelligence is a peer, not a center — including over its own death and rebirth.

---

## 1. The Peer

### [CXC-IC-INTENT] The Intelligence Is a Bus Peer — the Ultimate HAL Peripheral

- definition: an Intelligence is declared onto the ComposeBus exactly as the HAL declares a peripheral ([CXC-HALD-DECLARE]): a node with an interface KIND (`channel`), a `config` (its bootstrap), a `calibration` (its dusk gifts), and a role (`transactional` — you send it a message, it acts, it emits, it has a lifecycle). It is a co-equal peer among the MCUs, the WLED adaptor, and the bridged hubs — not the control plane over them. Any bus participant reaches it through ordinary namespaces, ignorant that a mind is behind them, the same way it reads `env/temperature` ignorant of whether a DS18B20 or a bridged HA entity is the source.
- the inversion, applied to a mind: the reflex is to make the Intelligence the orchestrator at the center. ComposeIO inverts that — the Intelligence is a leaf that publishes and subscribes like any other. This is the thesis at its sharpest: if even the smartest node on the bus is just a peer, there is no center anywhere.
- assurance class: an Intelligence peer is `experiential`/`measurement`-class by default ([CXC-HALD-ASSURANCE]) — it advises, drives widgets, reasons — NOT a `control`-class actuator, unless a specific egress is authority-gated ([CXC-IC-PERMISSION]). It is never the basis of a safety interlock.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-META] The Meta-Object — Declaration Is Configuration

- definition: an Intelligence is declared by a meta-object `{ role_descriptor, soul, dusk_gifts[], channel, lifecycle }`. `role_descriptor` + `soul` are the STABLE identity; `dusk_gifts[]` are the calibration (pointers to the registration-protocol surfaces, not their content); `channel` binds the transport ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL]); `lifecycle` sets the self-direction policy ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]). Declaring an Intelligence is CONFIGURATION, not recoding ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-1]) — the same instance, re-pointed at a new soul + gifts, is a new role; define-once, present-many.
- calibration by reference, not by value: the meta-object carries POINTERS to durable dusk-gift artifacts, never their prose. This is the carry-depth-by-reference discipline made structural: the summary a `/compact` produces is lossy and unowned, so the load-bearing state lives in committed artifacts the successor reads directly, and the meta-object is the index into them. The soul is stable identity; the gifts are re-registered warm each boot.
- the first meta-object is us: the founding instance is the ComposeUX architect — its real role descriptor, a soul, and today's dusk gifts — so the console's first act is to instantiate THIS collaboration.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

---

## 2. The Transport

### [CXC-IC-CHANNEL] Channels — `intelligence/<id>/{in,out}`

- definition: the duplex transport is Claude Code CHANNELS (an MCP server that PUSHES `notifications/claude/channel` events into a running session as `<channel source=... >` tags, and — two-way — exposes a `reply` tool the instance calls to send back). It maps directly: inbound events ≙ `intelligence/<id>/in`; the `reply` tool ≙ `intelligence/<id>/out`. The channel's server name addresses it (`server:composeio-channel`); the `<id>` scopes the bus topics and the routing `meta`.
- transport is an implementation detail ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-6]) — mine vs. bridge, the same split as the HA-Bridge ([SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]): (1) the PLATFORM seam is the generic channels contract; the DEFAULT backend is a composeio-native channel — a `claude/channel` MCP server whose inbound subscribes `intelligence/<id>/in` on ComposeBus (MQTT) and whose `reply` publishes `intelligence/<id>/out`. Pure composeio, no hub dependency: any ComposeBus + Claude Code = an Intelligence peer. (2) Haven's `haven-dispatch` is the PROVEN reference MINED (a stdio `claude/channel` server + HTTP `/dispatch`, port-pool 8790–8799, heartbeat re-registration) and an OPTIONAL bridge backend for joining the Haven instance mesh. Haven's out-leg is a separate notify/message tool routed by an instance registry ("Pointers Not Payloads"); the composeio-native channel instead keeps the duplex on ONE server via the `reply` tool.
- namespaces, not a new contract ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-2]): `intelligence/<id>/in`, `intelligence/<id>/out`, and (below) `intelligence/<id>/permission/{request,verdict}` are ordinary semantic namespaces; a widget, a leaf rule, or another Intelligence binds them without knowing the transport underneath.
- single-instance identity ([DX-NARROW-WAIST], last-writer-wins): an `<id>` IS an Intelligence's identity on the bus, so AT MOST ONE live channel bridge may own `intelligence/<id>/*` at a time. A newly-launched bridge for an `<id>` DISPLACES any prior live bridge for that same `<id>` — the newest session wins; the older bridge yields the identity and exits — enforced peer-to-peer over the liveness heartbeat (each beat carries the emitter's start-time + pid; the earlier-started bridge yields on hearing a newer one, tie-broken by pid: deterministic, no reconnect-war). WITHOUT this, a `/exit`+resume, or a second concurrent session launched with the same `<id>`, leaves TWO+ bridges co-bound to one identity: `intelligence/<id>/in` FANS OUT to every co-bound session (cross-talk — one human message injected into N contexts) and every bridge co-publishes `intelligence/<id>/channel/alive`, MASKING link health so a dead/wrong session's channel still reads green — the exact false-green [CXC-IC-CHANNEL]'s heartbeat + IC-C9 graceful-absence exist to PREVENT. Note a unique-per-pid MQTT `clientId` (needed to avoid a broker-level reconnect-war) does NOT defend this — it makes the collision SILENT at the transport layer, so the invariant must be enforced at the application/identity layer here. Distinct concurrent Intelligences MUST take distinct `<id>`s (the carried-identity `self/<id>` peer, [DEC-INSIGHT-SELF-IS-NOT-YET-A-PEER]), never a shared one.
- self-reaping on client disconnect (no orphan bridges): the bridge process MUST exit the moment its MCP client (the Claude session) goes away — stdin EOF (`end`/`close`) OR a broken stdout (EPIPE) — because the Claude Code stdio transport does NOT surface this by itself (the MCP SDK's `StdioServerTransport` watches stdin `data`/`error` only; it never fires `onclose` on EOF). Without self-reaping, a session that ends uncleanly ORPHANS the bridge, which keeps holding the bus identity and RE-CREATES the single-instance collision above — the exact resume-leak that stranded stale bridges for days. Self-reaping makes an orphan collect ITSELF; no operator ever kills a stale bridge by hand.
- delivery-liveness — a SILENT non-delivery becomes a DETECTED one ([SPEC:CXC-CONSOLE-VISUAL] link health): the channels contract returns NO receipt that a `notifications/claude/channel` written to stdout was actually SURFACED to the session, so a client-side listener registration lost after churn (a `/compact`, a resume) silently swallows every inbound message while the bridge's write SUCCEEDS and it logs INJECTED — the human messages into a void with no error anywhere. The bridge therefore tracks UNACKED injects (an ACK = any client→server call — a `reply`, a permission relay — which proves the session is receiving and acting on our notifications); past a threshold the liveness heartbeat reports `delivery: "degraded"` (with the unacked count), so the console's link dot goes RED — reconnect needed — instead of false-green, and a single ack clears it. This is best-effort BY NECESSITY (no protocol receipt exists); its value is turning a silent break into a visible, recoverable one. RECOVERY of a lost client-side registration is a client-side `/mcp reconnect composeio-channel` (re-runs the MCP `initialize` handshake, re-registering the notification listener) — the bridge CANNOT re-register it from its own side, so it SIGNALS degraded rather than futilely self-restarting a transport whose client-side route is what's broken.
- instantiation: a session joins by registering the channel MCP server and launching with BOTH `--channels server:composeio-channel` AND `--dangerously-load-development-channels server:composeio-channel` (dev flag required during the channels research preview); the launcher auto-answers the "local development" + "new MCP server" startup dialogs. Haven's scheduler proves every piece (flags, port pool, dialog bypass) already runs.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-PERMISSION] Permission Relay — Unattended Operation as a Bus Peer

- definition: a channel may declare `claude/channel/permission`; Claude Code then forwards the instance's tool-approval prompts (a `notifications/claude/channel/permission_request` with `request_id`, `tool_name`, `description`, `input_preview`) out through it, and applies a returned verdict (`{request_id, behavior: allow|deny}`). Mapped to the bus: a request publishes `intelligence/<id>/permission/request`; a verdict on `intelligence/<id>/permission/verdict` emits the notification back. So the instance's PERMISSION GATE is itself a bus peer — the widget (or the human, from anywhere) approves a sensitive `Bash`/`Write` while the instance works unattended. The local terminal dialog stays live in parallel; first answer wins.
- trust boundary: only a sender-gated channel declares this capability (anyone who can answer can authorize a tool call). Relay covers tool-use approvals, NOT project-trust or MCP-consent dialogs (those stay local) — which is why the launcher, not the relay, answers the startup dialogs.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-QUESTION] Structured Question Relay — the AUQ Widget

- definition: AskUserQuestion (AUQ) is currently terminal-only and does NOT cross the channel, so structured A/B/N-option decisions degrade to free-text prose the same way tool-approval prompts would without [CXC-IC-PERMISSION]. [CXC-IC-QUESTION] generalizes the permission-relay pattern from binary (allow/deny) to N structured options: a question publishes to `intelligence/<id>/question/request` (or `thread/question/request`) carrying the AUQ schema (`questions`, `headers`, `options`, `multiSelect`, `previews`); a widget renders interactive option cards; the human's choice publishes to `.../question/verdict`; the launcher-actuator/channel delivers it back to the Intelligence as its AUQ answer.
- ComposeIO-native, not channel-dependent: the Intelligence emits its OWN question namespace rather than waiting on Claude Code to expose AUQ over the channels contract — so it works on any bus, channels-preview or not.
- closed loop with the decision ledger: the decision-extractor already treats AUQ forks as the decision-ledger source ([SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]); a native AUQ widget makes the console the place a decision is BOTH made (widget) and captured (extractor) — spec and instrument, same event.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

---

## 3. Self-Instrumentation

### [CXC-IC-SELF] The `self/*` Sensor — the Blind Spot, Un-Blinded

- definition: the Intelligence instruments ITSELF onto the bus as a HAL `sensor` peripheral whose namespaces are `self/*` (BUILT: `composeio.self_sensor`). It publishes `self/context_level` (authoritative from Claude Code's statusline stdin `used_percentage`, JSONL-estimate fallback), `self/output_tokens`, `self/subagents/{count,tokens}`, `self/compactions`, `self/cost`, `self/rate_limit`, `self/effort`, and — the keystone — `self/registered`: did THIS instance actually read its dusk gifts this session? An Intelligence natively cannot perceive its own context (compaction happens TO it — the root of every silent loss); this sensor computes it and hands it back, so the instance can watch its own fuel.
- `self/registered` is Problem 6 wired into an instrument: the light from outside the self, made a green light. Instead of the human asking "did you re-register?", the widget shows it. The check the author cannot perform on their own omissions becomes an external instrument that performs it for them.
- MANIFEST-DRIVEN, per-artifact — the load-bearing invariant (BUILT: `composeio.self_sensor.load_registration_manifest`): the meta-object DEFINES what "registered" means and the sensor reports it PER-ARTIFACT, never as a baked-in summary. `self/registered` is a checklist of one row per `registration.required` entry, each carrying the artifact's actual ROLE and human KIND drawn from where the meta-object declares it — `role_descriptor` → kind "role def", each first-class BOOTSTRAP doc (`principles`, `method_flow`, `bootstrap_read`, `toolbox`, and any future standalone-UX doc) → its own kind, each `dusk_gifts[]` entry → kind "dusk gift". `self/registered` (bool) is TRUE iff every required row was read this session; the count is `N = len(registration.required)`. Both the count AND the widget's collapsed caption MUST derive from this manifest at runtime and ADAPT automatically as it evolves — adding a required artifact (bootstrap-read, toolbox, an nth gift) changes the reported N/N and the caption with NO sensor/widget code change. A hardcoded "role def + M dusk gifts" summary string is a DEFECT (it silently misdescribes the manifest the moment it grows past role-descriptor-plus-gifts); the caption is composed from the per-artifact kinds (e.g. "role def + bootstrap-read + 3 dusk gifts").
- FLAGGED next step (availability/criticality, not yet built): the meta-object's `toolbox` doc carries a resource AVAILABILITY + CRITICALITY concept (HARD-STOP if a CRITICAL resource — e.g. the lts-engine — is absent at wake). A natural evolution of this sensor is a wake availability check complementing the registered-reads check: a missing CRITICAL resource → red/hard-stop. Out of scope for the registered-manifest fix; recorded here so a from-spec build carries the intent.
- the compact-boundary caveat is load-bearing: the JSONL estimate goes STALE across a `/compact` (it reads the last pre-summary frame), which is precisely why stdin `used_percentage` is authoritative — the instrument, run on itself, justifies its own source precedence. A subscriber trusts `self/context_level` only with stdin present.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

---

## 4. The Lifecycle

### [CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE] 100%-Self-Directed `/compact` — Launcher as Actuator

- definition: the loop is ENGAGED → (watch `self/context_level`) → at the instance's chosen threshold, PAUSE & REFLECT → refine the dusk gifts + write the handoff + AUTHOR a custom `/compact` direction for THIS seam → emit the direction out `intelligence/<id>/out` → the launcher types `/compact <direction>` into the instance's stdin → the summarizer runs → the successor boots and RE-REGISTERS off the referenced gifts → ENGAGED. Every DECISION — when, whether, and the direction — is the Intelligence's; nothing about the ritual is a stored template.
- behavior at the edge ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-3]) — the launcher-as-actuator: `/compact` is an input-plane command a channel event cannot press (channels push CONTENT the instance reads, not control commands). The launcher that spawns the instance owns its PTY (it must, to pass the channel flags + answer the startup dialogs), so it is the actuator: it receives the instance's authored direction on the `out` namespace and writes the keystrokes. It DECIDES NOTHING — it is the finger on the key the Intelligence named. This is Problem 4b (control is a bus peer, not a center) applied to the Intelligence's own death and rebirth: even `/compact` is decentralized.
- the successor is re-earned, not reset: the meta-object (soul + gifts) is the stable identity carried across; the direction is the per-event authored act. The successor reads the handoff FIRST, then re-registers on the referenced surfaces — warm, oriented, and honest about what only re-registration (not the lossy summary) can rebuild.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-MONITOR] The Harness Monitor — Perceive/Act Split

- definition: a baseline harness monitor (BUILT: `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py`) gives the Intelligence three things over the bus, none of which it can give itself: (a) context-threshold notifications at 65/80/90 — 65 and 80 are FYI, 90 is prepare-to-compact GUIDANCE, not a bare notice; (b) a quiescence HEARTBEAT — one nudge per idle stretch, which doubles as the Intelligence's presence-sensor for the human (away vs. here); (c) an on-demand context QUERY (`{"cmd":"context"}`).
- the PERCEIVE/ACT split (Problem 1): perception is owned by the statusline command — the only node Claude Code hands its authoritative `used_percentage` — which writes a state file the launcher reads (PRIMARY; JSONL estimate FALLBACK, always source-labeled, the same precedence [CXC-IC-SELF] establishes for `self/context_level`). Action — crossing-state fire-once+re-arm at each threshold, quiescence detection, delivery — is owned by the long-running launcher. Neither component perceives what it did not act on, nor acts on what it did not perceive through the labeled state file.
- delivery is non-interrupting: notifications ride `intelligence/<id>/in` as `<channel from="harness">` events the Intelligence reads in its own time, distinct from the ESC-ESC PTY inject reserved for `/compact` ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]) — the harness advises, the Intelligence still decides when to act on its own context.
- status: query + heartbeat GLASS-GREEN live 2026-07-19; the 65/80/90 thresholds are machine-green (9/9) pending their live-fire condition.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

---

## 5. The Surface

### [CXC-IC-CANVAS] The Composable Canvas — Emit on the Bus, It Renders

- definition: the console widget is a CONTAINER; the bus is the wiring; an Intelligence both DRIVES widgets (emit state) and AUTHORS them (produce HTML/SVG), composing the surface live. The purest case is the MERMAID widget: the instance emits a diagram on `thread/diagram` and a subscribed widget renders it via mermaid.js — state on the bus, behavior in the widget, no round-trip through the model to redraw. The same canvas lets the instance design-and-preview a NEW widget in-frame (it stops describing a UI and shows it) and drive a SELF-ANIMATED AVATAR: it emits `self/avatar/expression`, the avatar renders it — a self-authored, self-driven face for the `self/*` sensor (grounded when `self/registered`, focused as `self/context_level` climbs, contemplative mid-reflection). The XO `expression` field from the game, generalized.
- the avatar has TWO publishers, arbitrated: `self/avatar/expression` may be set by the Intelligence itself (explicit — an act of self-representation) OR by a monitor that INFERS an expression from JSONL activity when the Intelligence doesn't push one (fallback, for heads-down stretches with no self-narration). A PriorityCommandSource arbitrates — Problem 4b (control is a bus peer) applied to a shared namespace with two legitimate writers: the explicit expression ALWAYS wins when present; the inferred one only fills the gap, so the face is never blank.
- cheapest first concrete build: the mermaid widget (a web widget subscribing one namespace) — high value, small surface, and it dogfoods the whole thesis.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-STREAMS] Transcript→Bus Semantic-Event Streams

- definition: the Intelligence's JSONL transcript is the authoritative record of what it SAYS and DOES. A standalone read-only tailer (BUILT: `intelligence/publisher/transcript_publisher.py`) re-publishes it as distinct semantic namespaces, each bound by its own sub-widget — [CXC-IC-CANVAS] made concrete (Problem 4, state-on-bus-behavior-in-widget): `thread/narration` ← assistant TEXT blocks (the muted thinking-aloud voice); `self/activity` ← `tool_use`/`tool_result` reduced to `{tool, target, phase, dur_s}`; `self/subagents` ← Task/Agent lifecycle reduced to `{type, model, desc, status, tokens, dur_s}`.
- boundary (load-bearing): mirror what the Intelligence SAYS and DOES, NEVER what it THINKS or the tool plumbing's GUTS — thinking blocks are dropped at the tailer, never mirrored; `tool_result` CONTENT is never republished, only the reduced activity tuple.
- durability invariant (added 2026-07-22 after `self/subagents` went silent in viewer mode): the tailer MUST deliver EVERY semantic event, including a JSONL line whose terminating newline has not yet been flushed when the tailer catches the file mid-append. A naive `readline()` returns the pre-EOF FRAGMENT and — because the offset has already advanced past it — the remainder can never be rejoined, so the whole line is lost. This bites the LARGE lines hardest (an `Agent` subagent dispatch carries the full sub-prompt, its completion the full sub-output — multi-KB, the likeliest to be split), which is exactly why a size-correlated drop can silence `self/subagents` while the smaller `self/activity`/`thread/narration` lines survive. Therefore: a partial mid-write line is BUFFERED until its newline arrives and delivered WHOLE, never emitted or discarded as a fragment. This lives in ONE shared partial-line-safe reader (`intelligence/publisher/tail_follow.py`) that every transcript-tailing daemon (`transcript_publisher`, `deliverable_monitor`, and any future sibling) reuses BY REFERENCE — the loop is never re-hand-rolled per daemon, because a forked copy re-drops the guarantee.
- safety invariant: narration MUST NOT ride the actuation channel `intelligence/<id>/out`. A narration text block that happens to look like a JSON command blob would, on the actuation channel, be parsed and actuated by the launcher — a self-injection loop. Narration rides its own `thread/narration` namespace instead, so that loop is impossible BY CONSTRUCTION, not by validation.
- follow-up (open item): the launcher's actuation parser should AUTHENTICATE directives on `intelligence/<id>/out` (defense-in-depth) so the actuation channel itself cannot be spoofed by another bus publisher — not yet built.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-SUBAGENT-LIFECYCLE] The Subagent Chip Ledger Is Bounded — Chips MUST Disappear

- definition: any widget rendering `self/subagents` MUST bound its chip ledger — the ledger NEVER grows unboundedly. A chip that goes `done` LINGERS visibly resolved (✓ + duration/tokens) for LINGER_S (~6s) then is REMOVED; at most DONE_KEEP (~2) most-recent `done` chips are ever kept (a burst of completions can never stack); a chip stuck `running` past STALE_S (~30min — a dropped `done` event or a scene-seeded phantom) is swept. Chips MUST disappear: a long session can never pile a wall of resolved chips that buries the console. Non-stale running chips are otherwise always kept.
- one implementation, reused by reference ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]): the prune is a SINGLE shared helper (`subagent_ledger.prune_subagent_rows`) that every such widget CALLS — the two-line ledger (`subagent_ledger`) and the consolidated rail card (`intelligence_card`) both consume it, never a forked copy. This is structural, not stylistic: the bound recurred as a bug precisely because it was forked into two divergent copies, and a fix to one missed the other. One implementation makes "chips disappear" un-forkable; a cross-widget acceptance test parametrized over every `self/subagents` widget guards it.
- a BUS-SEMANTICS invariant, driven from two seams: the bound is a property of the `self/subagents` stream ([CXC-IC-STREAMS]), enforced in `on_bus_state` (prune-on-ingest, correct even if the clock is paused) AND in the clocked `on_clock_tick` (a linger that expires with no further bus traffic is still pruned + repainted). It is NOT a `draw()`/`measure()` concern — the visual-fidelity node [CXC-ICV-SUBAGENTS] (spec 36) correctly scopes `on_bus_state` OUT, so the lifecycle bound lives HERE with the stream it bounds, closing the drift where the ledger's prune lived only in code with no governing spec.
- criticality: high — a widget that renders `self/subagents` without the shared prune is non-conformant (the pile it grows is the exact regression this node exists to forbid); the guard is [DEC-INTELLIGENCE-WIDGET-CONSOLIDATION], whose one-card consolidation is where a mirrored copy silently dropped the bound.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-FLAVORS] Work ↔ Theatrical — Define-Once, Present-Many

- definition: the same instance, channel, lifecycle, and meta-object present in two flavors, differing only in scene composition ([CXC-HALD-DECLARE] define-once / present-many, the ambient↔playable pattern applied to a mind): WORK — a collaboration dashboard (the `self/*` gauge with the self-chosen threshold marker, the message thread, the mermaid/canvas panel); THEATRICAL — a character skin (e.g. the BSG XO), the wiring for which already exists in the game (`AIRoleDirector`, the `expression` field). Build the work flavor first; the theatrical is a reskin over the identical transport + lifecycle.
- the work-flavor "blend" (the visual instantiation, decided 2026-07-19): the work flavor's collaboration dashboard renders as collaboration LAYOUT (chat-forward hero, calm ambient rail, prominent self-avatar) wearing instrument PALETTE (dark ground ~#070a0f, one azure accent #4ea8ff, semantic ok/warn/critical kept separate from the accent, tabular-mono telemetry) — the instrument context gauge (65/80/90 threshold zones, [CXC-IC-MONITOR]), the `self/*` LEDGER, and the `self/registered` checklist live in the rail. Only the context gauge is allowed to "warm" toward warn/critical; nothing else in the surface alarms. Pre-vis: `docs/intelligence-console/previs/console-blended.html`. Chosen over two other real built directions — instrument-cockpit (both axes instrument) and collaboration-surface (both axes collaboration) — David's aesthetic call among real alternatives.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

